Chapter: ProbeClosureOverapply

 Finding 39, entered through a FLAT OVER-APPLICATION rather than through
 a two-step under-application. Four lines, three definitions, and bare
 metal faults on the second one.

 `pick` returns a function and has two return paths and no recursion --
 deliberately the shape of tier 14's `control-pick`, which ANSWERS. The
 difference is only how the result is applied: tier 14 reaches it as
 `(pick 0) 2 3` inside a definition the pipeline inlines, and this
 applies the named definition flat at three arguments where it declares
 one.

 Measured 2026-08-25 against seed 6CF4A8E0:

   full: 6              both arms agree
   result-over: 6       bare metal FAULTS here; the zig arm answers 6
   result-over2: 7      never reached on bare metal; zig answers 7
   after: 15            never reached on bare metal; zig answers 15

 A sibling shape, `let p = pick 0 in show ((p 2) 3)`, does the SILENT
 thing instead: bare metal prints 6291624 -- 0x600028, a heap address --
 where 6 belongs, and faults on the line after. That is finding 39's own
 signature (it records 6291488 = 0x600020 in another program), reached
 from a different direction.

 This matters to `codex/plugs/test-input/partial.codex`, whose only
 definition returns an Integer, so the Rulebook's over-application case
 is unreachable from it. Every shape that WOULD reach it needs a
 definition returning a function, and every such shape lands here.


  add3 : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  add3 (a) (b) (c) = a + b + c

  pick : Integer -> (Integer, Integer -> Integer)
  pick (n) = if n == 0 then add3 1 else add3 2

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
   print-line-uni ("full: " & show (add3 1 2 3))
   print-line-uni ("result-over: " & show (pick 0 2 3))
   print-line-uni ("result-over2: " & show (pick 1 2 3))
   print-line-uni ("after: " & show (add3 4 5 6))
  end
